    "# Data Report\n",
    "\n",
    "This data report documents all datasets used in the project in order to ensure transparency, traceability and reproducibility. Since the project combines multiple statistical sources to visualise different dimensions of the generational conflict in Switzerland, particular attention is paid to the origin, structure, quality and transformation of the data.\n",
    "\n",
    "The report distinguishes between raw data and processed data. Raw data refers to the original files as obtained from public statistical sources. Processed data refers to cleaned, harmonised and transformed datasets that are used directly in the Streamlit application. This distinction is important because the original data sources often differ in structure, naming conventions, temporal coverage and level of aggregation.\n",
    "\n",
    "The purpose of this documentation is not only to describe the data technically, but also to make methodological decisions explicit. This includes the harmonisation of age groups, the selection of relevant indicators, the treatment of missing values and the limitations resulting from aggregated public data.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Raw Data\n",
    "\n",
    "The raw datasets are documented according to the three main dimensions of the project: **Who decides?**, **Who pays?**, and **Who owns?**. This thematic structure follows the logic of the final visualisation and makes it transparent which data sources are used for each analytical perspective.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Overview Raw Datasets\n",
    "\n",
    ": Overview of raw datasets used in the project. {#tbl-raw-overview}\n",
    "\n",
    "| Analytical dimension | Dataset name | Source | Storage location |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Who pays? | Mandatory health insurance statistics (OKP/KVSTAT) | Swiss health insurance statistics, annual Excel files and ZIP archives | `data/whopays/raw_whopays/` |\n",
    "| Who pays? | AHV income data | Project-specific AHV income data file | `data/whopays/raw_whopays/ahv_einnahmen_zhaw.xlsx` |\n",
    "| Who pays? | AHV expenditure data | Swiss official statistics export | `data/whopays/raw_whopays/px-x-1305000000_103_20260416-235225.xlsx` |\n",
    "| Who pays? | Population by age and year | Swiss official population statistics export | `data/raw/px-x-0102020000_103_20260424-164251.xlsx` |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Details Raw Data: Who Decides – Political Participation and Voting Behaviour\n",
    "\n",
    "The raw data for the analytical dimension **\"Who decides?\"** consists of three post-election survey datasets from the Swiss Electoral Study (SELECTS), covering the federal elections of 2015, 2019 and 2023. This dimension focuses on political participation and democratic attitudes across age groups and generations in Switzerland. The aim is to analyse whether different generations participate equally in democratic decision-making processes and how the composition of the active electorate has shifted over time.\n",
    "\n",
    "The three SELECTS datasets are post-election surveys conducted after each federal election by FORS (Swiss Centre of Expertise in the Social Sciences) at the Université de Lausanne. They are available through the FORS data archive and are distributed under a standard research data licence that permits use for non-commercial scientific and educational purposes. No individual-level personal data is disclosed: respondents are identified only by survey ID, and sensitive attributes such as birth year are available only as aggregated or derived variables.\n",
    "\n",
    "The raw datasets were accessed and processed in three separate files:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `data/whodecide/raw/726_Selects2015_PES_Data_v1.03.dta` — SELECTS 2015 raw data in Stata format\n",
    "- `data/whodecide/processed/selects_2015_clean.csv` — Preprocessed version of the 2015 data\n",
    "- `data/whodecide/processed/selects_2019_clean.csv` — Preprocessed 2019 data\n",
    "- `data/whodecide/processed/selects_2023_clean.csv` — Preprocessed 2023 data\n",
    "\n",
    "The raw SELECTS files are not directly suitable for visualisation because variable names, coding conventions and value ranges differ across survey waves. Variable names follow the standardised SELECTS naming scheme (e.g. `f11100rec`, `f10100`, `f12010`, `f12900rec`) but their internal coding and valid value ranges differ between years. In particular, the left–right self-placement variable uses a 7-point scale in 2015 (`f12900main7`, values 1–7) but a 5-point recoded scale in 2019 and 2023 (`f12900rec`, coded as 10/20/30/40/50). Substantial preprocessing was therefore required to harmonise the three waves into a consistent analytical structure before generation assignment and aggregation.\n",
    "\n",
    "From a data governance perspective, the SELECTS datasets are public-use research files distributed by FORS under standard terms of use for academic research. They do not contain individually identifiable data. The datasets are widely used in Swiss political science and are considered reliable reference data for electoral behaviour research. The processed outputs derived from these datasets are therefore best understood as analytical summaries of survey-reported behaviour by generation, not as administrative records of individual votes.\n",
    "\n",
    "The relevance of this data for the visualisation dimension \"Who decides?\" is direct: the SELECTS surveys are the primary empirical source for measuring voter turnout, political interest, democratic satisfaction and political orientation (left–right self-placement) in Swiss federal elections. By linking survey respondents to their birth year and assigning them to generational groups, the project can show how these four indicators differ across generations and how they have changed over three election cycles (2015, 2019, 2023).\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Data Catalogue\n",
    "\n",
    "The data catalogue below describes the harmonised schema produced by the preprocessing pipeline (`who_decides_section.py`). All three processed CSV files share this schema after loading and standardisation. The original SELECTS variable names are retained where possible to ensure traceability back to the source codebooks.\n",
    "\n",
