    "\n",
    "No single master table was created before the exploratory analysis because the OKP, AHV income and AHV expenditure datasets differ in structure, units, age coverage and interpretation. Keeping the analytical files separate reduces unnecessary complexity and makes methodological limitations more transparent.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Data Characteristics and Quality\n",
    "\n",
    "The exploratory analysis showed that the data for the **“Who pays?”** dimension is suitable for a high-level visualisation of generational financial burden, but only after substantial preprocessing and with several methodological limitations.\n",
    "\n",
    "The main quality issue is structural heterogeneity. The OKP/KVSTAT data is provided as a series of annual Excel files and ZIP archives whose internal structure changes over time. Sheet names, available tables and column positions are not fully consistent across years. For this reason, the data acquisition script uses separate extraction logic for different file groups and explicitly selects the relevant statistical tables. The main OKP tables used in the project include premium-related indicators, premium reductions, cost sharing, net benefits and gross benefits by age group.\n",
    "\n",
    "A second important issue is the harmonisation of age groups. The original OKP and AHV datasets are often provided in age cohorts rather than single-year ages. To make the data comparable across generations, these cohort-level values are disaggregated to single ages using population weights. The population data provides age-specific population counts by year. The pipeline calculates an average population value from the population at the beginning and end of the year and uses this as the weighting basis. This method improves comparability but remains an approximation: values within an age group are distributed proportionally to population size, not observed directly at individual age level.\n",
    "\n",
    "For the OKP data, the relevant variables include premiums, premium reductions, cost sharing, net benefits and gross benefits. These variables should not be interpreted as identical forms of financial burden. For example, a net health insurance burden would conceptually require combining several components, such as premiums, premium reductions and cost sharing. Therefore, visualisations based only on premiums show the payment side of the system, but not necessarily the full net burden after subsidies.\n",
    "\n",
    "The AHV income data has an important limitation: it only covers employees. Self-employed persons and non-employed persons are not included in the age-specific AHV income dataset. This means that the calculated number of contributors and contribution sums underestimate the full AHV contribution base. The macro-level dataset `ahv_makro.csv` is therefore used as a plausibility check against official aggregate contribution totals. In the exploratory analysis, the deviation between the employee-based dataset and the macro-level control values was consistent and plausible. This supports the use of the data for trend analysis, while still requiring clear documentation of the missing contributor groups.\n",
    "\n",
    "The AHV expenditure data has a different structure. It covers pension recipients and therefore mainly concerns persons aged 65 and older. Younger age groups do not appear in the old-age pension data because they do not receive regular AHV old-age pensions. This is not a data error, but a structural characteristic of the Swiss old-age pension system. The variable `renten_mittelwert_chf` represents a monthly pension value, not an annual value, and must therefore not be divided by twelve during analysis.\n",
    "\n",
    "The comparison between AHV contributions and AHV benefits is analytically meaningful, but must be interpreted carefully. Contribution values describe what an individual contributor or contributor group pays into the system, while pension values describe what a pension recipient receives. This reflects the logic of a pay-as-you-go pension system. The ratio between contributors and pension recipients is therefore an important contextual indicator. In the available data, the number of contributors per pension recipient decreased from approximately 3.49 in 2012 to approximately 3.22 in 2024. Since the contributor count only includes employees, the absolute level of this ratio is conservative, but the downward trend remains informative.\n",
    "\n",
    "Several generation-specific values require caution. Very young generations, especially Generation Alpha, should not be interpreted as economically meaningful AHV contributors, because children do not usually pay AHV contributions. Similarly, values for the Silent Generation on the contribution side represent only a very small remaining employed group. For this reason, the AHV contribution visualisation should focus primarily on economically active generations where the interpretation is meaningful.\n",
    "\n",
    "Overall, the data quality is sufficient for the intended visualisation goal: to show broad generational patterns in mandatory payments, social insurance contributions and public transfers. However, the results should be interpreted as aggregated and methodologically harmonised indicators, not as exact individual-level accounting. The main limitations are the use of population-weighted disaggregation, differences in temporal coverage, changes in raw file structure, missing contributor groups in the AHV income data and the different interpretation of payment-side and benefit-side indicators.\n",
